le in helping recommending the  right services to the right citizens  so this is very useful like you want to  really target your  your your benefits and your services  to the right people who really need it  and so it can be really useful to use  machine learning  to do this another another way it's  being used within government is to  prioritize cases like if you only have  resources  to  have to call ten percent of your citizen  to offer them a certain service  like which uh which 10 percent are going  to call actively  uh so it's very useful to know okay like  which citizen would be them  would be those who most likely need this  help so you can start to argue them  more efficiently and finally another use  case is simply  detecting fraud like you're working with  not tax payer money  so it is important to be able to spot  fraud  and using the pathway detection  [Music]  methods of machine learning can really  help in this and so this is another area  where you can use machine learning but  also maybe it can be problematic here to  use  use algorithms so often you hear this  and this this throat that these  algorithms are just  basically black boxes that will spit out  a prediction  but nobody really knows what's going on  inside them i don't think it's really  true anymore  but this is this one one of one of the  worries about using machine learning  algorithms  in a public sector so we know that  machine learning algorithms can be  extremely powerful in finding and  exploiting patterns to predict outcomes  which naturally begs the question uh  which pattern is it using is the right  patterns  and should be worried about like which  pattern patterns actually finding the  data  to make such accurate predictions um  and this and the and the fact that  people feel that they don't know exactly  what this model is doing  then it generates fear  uh so this models you know if you have a  random forest model or an actually boost  model  can consist of dozens or even hu